WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.135
-One of the ways that you have
spoken about paying

00:00:03.135 --> 00:00:04.971
for this bill is, of course,
taxing billionaires more.

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:06.305
-Yes.
-Are you worried --

00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:07.840
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.708
Bear with me here.
Are you worried,

00:00:09.708 --> 00:00:12.178
if you tax them too much,
they will stop going to space?

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:13.913
[ Laughter ]

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.949
-You know, it is something

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:19.819
that I've never even once
worried about.

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.087
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, okay.

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:23.123
-I can't even make up
a good lie about that.

00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:26.726
Are you kidding me?
I mean, for these guys

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:31.297
to shoot off into space,
people who are paying

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:36.502
less in taxes -- right? --
than public schoolteachers,

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:38.271
than firefighters,

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:41.006
than folks who are working hard
every day,

00:00:41.006 --> 00:00:43.476
and then to say, "Thank y'all,
because the way

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:47.480
I was able to do this
was by not paying taxes,"

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.215
I mean, could we be more overt

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.717
about what is broken
in this country?

00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:54.220
-It does strike me...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.689
...it is very overt.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:58.691
It's not much --
They make it real --

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.960
-This is, like, not subtle.
-No, it's true.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.697
-This is like when --
a cowboy hat on Lex Luthor.

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:06.332
-Here's how not-subtle it is.
They count it down.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:09.668
They want you to see it so bad,
they count it down.

00:01:09.668 --> 00:01:11.870
-Exactly.
-They're like, "10! 9!"

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.707
They're like, "Look!"
-Right, right, right, uh-huh.

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:16.476
-This is very exciting.
You wrote a book.

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:20.079
-I did.
-You have, obviously --

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:22.482
You've written books
for adults.

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:23.882
-Yeah.
-And I'm curious,

00:01:23.882 --> 00:01:26.786
is it just a super breeze
when you're writing for kids?

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:29.255
Be honest.
-It's just different.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:32.391
-Yeah. That's a very good --
I mean, again, these kids,

00:01:32.391 --> 00:01:33.526
they can't vote.
You can...

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:34.994
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.495
...say what you want about them.
They can't vote.

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:38.398
-No, no.
It's just different.

00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:40.566
I actually -- since I'd never
written a book for children,

00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:44.036
I actually took
photocopy paper.

00:01:44.036 --> 00:01:46.673
And I knew
what the story line was.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:48.575
I had the plot down...
-That's good.

00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:50.743
...and what I wanted to say,

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:55.080
and so I'd write it
and tape it on pages,

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.783
because you need the pages
to turn.

00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:00.052
It's got to be
a real page-turner, you know?

00:02:00.052 --> 00:02:04.357
[ Laughter ]
And it was fun, and I loved it.

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:08.128
I wrote this because I did it

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:11.864
about a zillion thousand
pinkie promises

00:02:11.864 --> 00:02:17.837
with little girls and
big girls and senior girls

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:21.541
and brothers and daddies
and dogs.

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:23.176
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:26.245
-This, we should note --
all pre-COVID, right?

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:27.980
-All pre-COVID.
-If you came at me

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.282
with a pinkie right now,
I would...

00:02:30.282 --> 00:02:33.385
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:36.189
But I really wanted
to think about

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:38.524
what all those pinkie promises
were about.

00:02:38.524 --> 00:02:42.394
And I wrote this --
You know, I ran for president

00:02:42.394 --> 00:02:43.930
and I lost.
And I wrote this

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.300
right after that,
because I really wanted

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:51.137
to say,
the most important thing is,

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:54.674
girls hear a lot
about what they can't do.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:56.809
Sometimes it's said out loud.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:03:00.146
Most of the time anymore,
it's said quietly, but there.

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:02.448
And what I really wanted to do

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:06.185
is say, "What matters is.
you get in the fight."

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.521
Yeah, I ran for president.
I lost.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:10.956
But ask me how sorry I am
that I ran.

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:15.628
Not one bit, not one bit.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:17.897
And that's what this is about.

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:20.633
-I, um...
-It's true.

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:25.438
-So, I want to play
a little quiz with you,

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:26.839
see how well you know
your own writing.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.007
-Okay.
[ Light laughter ]

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:30.076
-I'm gonna read lines
from your writing.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:33.212
It's either gonna be
some lines from the book...

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:34.980
-This book?
-This book.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.315
-"Pinkie Promises."
-...or some of your

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:39.552
academic writings on bankruptcy.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:41.754
-Okay.
Don't make this too hard.

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:43.856
And I just have to pick?
-You just have to say,

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:47.227
"That's from 'Pinkie Promises,"

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:49.062
or, "That's from
my bankruptcy writing."

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.664
[ Laughter ]
-Okay. Okay, I'm ready.

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:54.099
-"For every party helped
by recognition of a claim

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:56.502
that a debtor would have delayed
long enough under state law

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:57.870
to escape payment entirely,

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.138
and for every noncontract claim,

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:01.073
it was able to collect
a pro rata share

00:04:01.073 --> 00:04:02.408
that would have been otherwise

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:03.909
practically impossible
to collect,

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:05.244
there is some other party
who lost the benefits

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:07.480
enjoyed in the hierarchical
system of state law."

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.449
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:11.317
-Okay.
Like, I'm really embarrassed.

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:14.320
That came from my
not-"Pinkie Promises" writing.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.688
-That's right.
-Okay.

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:17.790
-Do you want to try one more?
-Oh, yeah. Sure.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.959
[ Light laughter ]

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.495
-"Monday was Polly's
first day at a new school."

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:24.563
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:26.132
[ cheers and applause ]

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:28.734
Even I knew that one.
Oh, no, no, no.

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:31.237
Did you get tested?
Did you pass?

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:32.772
-I did.
-Okay, I'll do one.

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:33.873
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:35.074
Senator Elizabeth Warren,
everybody!

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:36.576
"Pinkie promises"
is available now.

